  <h2 id="what-is-fps">What is Frame Rate (FPS)?</h2>
  <p>Video is essentially a series of still images played sequentially at high speed to create the illusion of motion. <strong>Frame rate</strong>, measured in Frames Per Second (FPS), dictates how many of these still images are displayed every second.</p>
  <p>The higher the frame rate, the smoother the motion appears. However, a higher frame rate isn't always "better"—different frame rates evoke different feelings and are used for different types of content.</p>

  <h2 id="24fps">24 FPS: The Cinematic Standard</h2>
  <p>Since the late 1920s, 24fps has been the global standard for cinema. It provides just enough frames for smooth motion while retaining a distinct "motion blur" that our brains associate with big-budget movies. If you watch a blockbuster film, it is almost certainly playing at 24fps. It feels natural, dramatic, and "filmic".</p>

  <h2 id="30fps">30 FPS: TV and Web Standard</h2>
  <p>30fps (often historically 29.97fps in North America) is the standard for live television broadcasts, news, soap operas, and the vast majority of web video (like standard YouTube videos or Instagram reels). It provides slightly smoother motion than film without looking unnaturally fast.</p>

  <h2 id="60fps">60 FPS: Gaming and Action</h2>
  <p>60fps is a high frame rate. Because it displays twice as much visual information per second as 30fps, motion appears incredibly smooth and hyper-realistic. </p>
  <p>It is the standard for video games (where responsiveness is key) and is often used in live sports broadcasts (to track fast-moving balls and athletes). However, when applied to a dramatic movie, 60fps can cause the "soap opera effect," making the film look cheap or like a home video.</p>
  
